SLICE = '''WS03 S8-B: crash-park. DESIGN ONLY, revision 4, made against branch model-plane at HEAD dfd1a8d5 (S8-A is built: commits c6a95ae0 .. 6ddfae42, ledger entry dfd1a8d5). `git status --short` shows only the untracked .plan-work/. Model Plane paths are under "apps/Model Plane"; every citation is a function or constant name, never a line number.

S8-B is: the session-core recovery worker PARKS an ACTIVE fold (a run whose executor crashed mid-loop) at its 15-minute recovery deadline instead of ending the run outcome_unknown, so that a resume through the S8-A park route (POST /continue with an empty approval_id and a claim token, AgentResume.mode "park") can continue it.

It changes three crates and nothing else: mp-contracts, session-core and execution-core, plus the compose file and the docs. There is NO migration, NO proto change, NO new wire token and NO model-gateway, BFF or SPA change: a crash-parked fold is an ordinary kind=park fold to the S8-A claim, the S8-A resume and the S8-A gateway regime, and RunDetail already projects park_reason crash_interrupted (it is in ParkReason::ALL).

S8-B v1 covers ONE class of run, on purpose: a pinned, root, persistent, read-only (plan_mode and autonomy rung read_only), built-in-tools-only run. That is not a small or empty set: it is every ChatGPT-subscription "Do" turn from the SPA (a subscription turn sends tools [], and the gateway maps plan_mode to the read_only rung) in a non-Space thread of an org with no registered MCP tool, and every API caller that sends a pinned plan_mode run with no client tools; validate_run_route refuses a pinned run without plan_mode unless the effects switch is on. It delivers nothing until something calls the resume route (open_questions). Everything else keeps the committed ~15 minute outcome_unknown. Extending it to effect-capable runs is listed as an owner decision with what it would need, not designed here.

Three design-review rounds (v2, v3 and v4 of the S8 design) each found a critical or high defect in the crash-park half. This revision answers the five that were named, concretely and without the machinery that produced new defects in v4: no thread watermark and no migration, no loop_checkpoint_superseded token, no heartbeat change, no claim grace, no third writer_gate.'''

SUMMARY = '''WHAT IT DOES
- The executor, behind its own default-off switch EXECUTION_LOOP_CRASH_PARK_ENABLED, writes ONE eligibility marker, the extension key crash_parkable whose value carries the route model, the connection id, the child ceiling and the parks count a resume needs, on every ROUND-BOUNDARY fold of a run that is crash-resumable AT REST. The S8-A park keys (parks, route_model, route_connection_id, child_ceiling) stay exclusive to park folds: session-core's re-seal derives them from the marker's payload. The marker is computed from facts of the run, never from a tool listing alone, and is re-checked by a pure function shared with session-core (mp_contracts::crash_park::crash_reseal), so the two sides cannot disagree about what a resumable fold is.
- session-core, behind its own default-off switch SESSION_CORE_CRASH_PARK_ENABLED (effective only with park recovery on), runs one new branch in recover_claimed_terminalization, after the S8-A park spare and before the terminal write: an active, marked, clean boundary fold of a run that never raised an approval and reserved no ledger row is re-sealed in place under seq+1 as kind=park, state=parked, park_reason crash_interrupted, due at once, and the run's obligation is handed back at the crash horizon. Everything else is terminalized exactly as committed.
- A crash park is held for its own, shorter TTL (SESSION_CORE_CRASH_PARK_TTL_SECONDS, default 3600, clamp 60 s to 30 days), not the park TTL, and the worker reads it whenever park recovery is active, so a crash park nothing resumes costs at most that long AFTER the 15-minute deadline (deadline plus TTL in all, not the TTL alone), not 24 hours, even after the crash switch is killed.
- Resume is the S8-A path, unchanged. A crash-parked fold passes check_park_fold by construction (and a test proves it).

THE ANSWERS TO THE FIVE DEFECTS
1. A crash park nothing can resume is held for the TTL: the marker means "park-resumable at rest". It is written only for a root, pinned, persistent, Space-less, read-only run whose offered tool names are derivable from the tools the loop really runs under (a direct name proof over ctx.allowlist, in addition to the request-kind fact) and which holds no release and was started fresh or by a park resume. An approval continuation (segment or in place), a delegated loop, a client- or MCP-tool run, an unpinned run, a ZDR or waived run never carries it. A deploy or config drift between crash and resume is the one residual: it costs at most the crash TTL.
2. A live-but-silent loop crash-parked under itself, two loops, two answers: the acts of a read-only loop that cannot be taken back are ending the run (its answer and its terminal receipt) and raising an approval, and those are fenced EXACTLY. A loop silent past the 900 s deadline also holds a dead 300 s session bearer by default, so its session-core writes are refused before any seq is compared; the design does not rely on that, it makes a Denied save mean 'cannot prove ownership: stop'. Before the acts of a marked loop that nothing can take back, ending the run (append its answer and write the terminal receipt, for ANY outcome) and raising an approval, the loop saves a TOMBSTONE fold ("answering" or "paused") through the fenced writer; if that save finds the seq moved (the worker re-sealed, a resume fenced), the run ended, or the store refuses this loop (Denied, which is what a loop silent past its 300 s session bearer gets), the loop stops and does neither. One funnel covers every exit that reaches finalize, typed failures included. What the loop cannot prove (a Denied or transient answer) it resolves with its own monotonic clock: it proceeds only while the last heartbeat the store accepted was sent less than 780 s ago, because the worker cannot act before 900 s have passed. The worker and the save take the same runs row lock and then the fold row lock, so either the tombstone commits first (the worker then sees a non-resumable fold and does not park) or the re-seal commits first (the tombstone save is stale and the loop never appends). Nothing depends on a clock, on the messages table or on append_message_in_tx's locks. A marked loop also stops at its next loop top after any definitive ownership failure (Stale, Closed, Denied, NotFound), so a superseded loop does not burn the rest of its budget.
3. The duplicate-answer guard and the two clocks: there is no clock comparison anywhere. messages.created_at (application clock) is never read; the guard is the ordering of the tombstone save and the append, serialized by the fold row's seq compare-and-set.
4. An old session-core with a new executor: the executor's marker keys are inert extension keys and its tombstone is an ordinary valid fold, so an old store stores them and never crash-parks. A session-core with S8-B and an executor without it sees unmarked folds and terminalizes as committed. Neither direction needs a handshake, because the marker is the executor's opt-in and the store's re-seal refuses any fold without it.
5. The kill and rollback order: crash-park off first (session-core), then the executor's marking, then the S8-A order; session-core below S8-B only after crash parks have drained; an executor below S8-A never while crash parks exist. Each is a row of the failure matrix.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE
- No effect-capable run is crash-parked (a crash makes their effects unknowable; only a read-only run provably has none).
- No thread notice on a park (owner decision, listed).
- No automatic resume and no UI button: crash-park is only worth flipping once something calls the resume route (owner decision, listed with the prerequisites).
- No SPA change: a crash-parked run stays 'running' for the deadline plus the crash TTL and sends no run_parked frame (the executor is dead), so the SPA follows it as waiting, settles 'unknown' after its own fixed 15 minutes and shows a waiting tail again on each reload. An SPA change that recognises a crash park is listed as a prerequisite the owner may set for the session-core switch.

SWITCHES (all default off, strict "true")
- EXECUTION_LOOP_CRASH_PARK_ENABLED (execution-core): marks eligible folds, saves the answering or paused tombstone before ending a run or raising an approval, stops a loop that cannot prove ownership. This is NOT an observation-only flip: a marked loop that finds the run ended (cancelled, terminalized by the worker, a duplicate dispatch) now stops without its late answer or receipt, where committed code appended it; a marked loop whose bearers expired but whose last heartbeat is recent keeps the committed immediate failed receipt. Owner-gated like the session-core switch. Needs the S8-A resume switches (EXECUTION_LOOP_RESUME_ENABLED, AGENT_CONTINUE_AFTER_APPROVAL_ENABLED, EXECUTION_LOOP_CHECKPOINT_ENABLED, EXECUTION_EFFECT_LEDGER_ENABLED); it does NOT need EXECUTION_LOOP_PARKING_ENABLED. It is also gated by a const (CRASH_RETIRE_BUILT) that stays false until the tombstone step has landed, so an image between the two steps cannot mark by configuration.
- SESSION_CORE_CRASH_PARK_ENABLED (session-core): the worker's crash-park. Effective only when park_recovery_active() is true.
- SESSION_CORE_CRASH_PARK_TTL_SECONDS (session-core): the wait of a crash park.

Flip order: the S8-A flips (1) to (3) with the S8-A park resume live-proven, the executor switch (owner-gated, its visible effects listed), then session-core's, last and owner-gated. Kill order is the reverse.'''

APPROACH = r'''0. READ THIS FIRST: WHAT THE TREE IS (verified at HEAD dfd1a8d5)

session-core
- loop_checkpoint::save locks the run (run_owner_tx, runs FOR UPDATE) and then the fold row (FOR UPDATE), compares seq (a mismatch is aborted "loop_checkpoint_stale", a closed row failed_precondition "loop_checkpoint_closed", an ended run "loop_checkpoint_run_ended"), seals under AAD seq and upserts. Park rules (validate_park_save, park_accepted) apply only under park_recovery_active(). It refuses park_reason crash_interrupted ("park_reason_reserved"); that stays.
- loop_checkpoint::claim with an empty pause_ref and park_recovery_active() runs CLAIM_PARK_SQL: kind='park', state 'parked' or 'resuming' under a lapsed lease, park_until <= now(); it never sets updated_at. A held lease of any token is claim_held; not due is park_not_due.
- terminalization::recover_claimed_terminalization takes lock_run_status (runs FOR UPDATE) and lock_managed_obligation (FOR UPDATE), checks the lease, a terminal run (supersede), has_pending_approval, then (S8-A) spare_parked_run (PARK_SPARE_HORIZON_SQL takes the fold row FOR UPDATE and spares a kind=park fold, parked or resuming, until updated_at + park TTL, handing the obligation back at LEAST(that, now + 900 s)), then PARK_HELD_SQL, then apply_managed_terminal_outcome (outcome_unknown, one receipt, the fold purged in the same transaction).
- heartbeat_managed_run_inner (runs, then the obligation) renews state 'open' and deadline_at = Utc::now() + 900 s for an open or processing obligation; it answers already_terminal for a terminal run. It is NOT touched by S8-B.
- run_loop_checkpoints (0047): one row per run, kind in round|pre_effect|pause|park, state in active|paused|parked|resuming|closed, seq, pause_ref, park_reason, park_until, authority_digest, segment_no, ciphertext, fold_digest, claim_token_hash, claim_expires_at, updated_at (the park's age and the TTL anchor).
- session_tool_audit_intents (0013 + 0048): (run_id, action_id) rows with status reserved|completed|failed|not_run|unknown_outcome and reserved_at; the executor reserves only effectful calls and a release (agent.rs, `entry.class.is_effectful() || is_release`).
- approvals (0003) has run_id and an index on it; a pinned run never gets a pause fold (resume::run_pause_mode returns "" for a non-empty provider_hint), so its approval is a cold one and the fold stays whatever the last round saved.
- the delegated bearers a run carries (session, inference, data plane) live 300 s by default (run_budget.rs; PLANE_TOKEN_TTL_* overrides), and a pinned run stops starting rounds 60 s before the earliest expires. The executor's heartbeat and its terminal receipt use fresh service tokens (session_terminal_auth.rs, terminal_tokens), NOT those bearers; fold saves, AppendMessage and every other session-core call of the loop use the delegated session bearer, so a loop that outlives it gets Unauthenticated on all of them (classify_status: Refused(Denied)) except those two.
- execution-core installs no metrics recorder (fold.rs record_metrics says so): its counters are no-ops today and its info! lines are the evidence. session-core installs a Prometheus recorder.
- the descriptor cipher is built on demand with DescriptorCipher::from_env() (the recovery worker has none today); seal and open take an AAD scope, scope_aad(run, org, user, seq).

execution-core
- run_rounds: the ONE writer_gate; the loop top checks ctx.route.is_stopped(), the latched refusal (park site A), the deadline (park site B), then heartbeat_managed_agent_run(..).await? (any heartbeat error aborts the loop with Err, before finalize). A round's fold is saved best effort at the END of a round that ran tools (folds.save_round); a round that ends in an answer saves nothing. An effectful root call is preceded by folds.save_pre_effect (fail closed: Stale, Closed, Refused, Unavailable all HOLD the effect). FoldWriter::apply moves Stale and Closed to State::Failed and the loop goes on.
- finalize: when !req.zdr && refusal.is_none() it appends the assistant message (append_message), then record_managed_agent_terminal_outcome. Two RPCs.
- fold.rs: build_fold writes only extension effect_ledger; Snapshot is the loop's locals; FoldWriter::{seeded,new,save_round,save_pre_effect,save_fence,save_park,pause_fold}; save_tombstone_as is private; Header::tombstone makes a fold with no conversation and extension not_resumable.
- park.rs (S8-A): StaticFacts / run_parkable (includes parking_active()), ParkStop, try_park, park_fold (the ONLY writer of EXT_PARKS, EXT_ROUTE_MODEL, EXT_ROUTE_CONNECTION, EXT_CHILD_CEILING, pinned by park_writer_violations), is_park_resume, admit, derivable_tool_defs, claim_parked, check_park_fold (14 rules), route_request, fence_parked.
- agent.rs: offered_tools decides derivability by REQUEST KIND (a request with a resume is never derivable; a fresh one is derivable when nothing a caller or an MCP server added is in the set and the server-resolved view was read); run_agent_with_route_parkable sets LoopContext.tools_derivable = derivable && no release, builds the allowlist from the tools the loop really runs under (after a continuation's snapshot replaced them), and park_continuation gives a park resume the derived set.
- The permission pre-pass in run_rounds rejects, at EVERY depth, a call whose rung the run was not granted (permission::check_autonomy_rung) and, in plan mode, a risky call; effect_class_of derives from the same permission::rung_required_for, so a run granted read_only cannot run a call the ledger classes as effectful. A delegated loop (depth > 0) has no fold writer.
- model-gateway: an executor Err(Aborted) or Err(Unavailable without a connect-phase io error) is neither a confirmed rejection nor "never reached", so a fresh run's stream answers agent_dispatch_unavailable plus RUN_DISPATCH_DEGRADED and terminalizes nothing; a park continue goes through park_dispatch_of and never terminalizes.

1. THE DESIGN IN ONE PAGE

(a) ELIGIBILITY AT REST (executor, marker). On every boundary fold a marked run writes, the executor adds ONE extension key, crash_parkable, whose value is {v:1, model, connection_id, child_ceiling, parks}: the identifiers a resume needs, content-free. It does not write the S8-A park keys (parks, route_model, route_connection_id, child_ceiling): those stay exclusive to park_fold, which keeps S8-A's guard on them untouched, and crash_reseal derives them from the payload. A fold is "at a boundary" when round_in_flight is None and pending is None; a pre_effect fold of an open round, a pause fold and a park fold never carry the marker. The marker is written only when ALL of these hold at that save: crash_marking_active() (the switch, the const CRASH_RETIRE_BUILT, and the resume half: park_resume_built() && park_resume_active()), the S8-A static facts minus the parking switch (depth 0, not zdr, not waived, no Space, tools_derivable by request kind, holds no release, seed Fresh or Parked, pinned, connection id and model present, restated as run_crash_parkable's own conjunct list, never by splitting run_parkable), the run is READ-ONLY (req.plan_mode and the autonomy rung is read_only), the tool NAMES the loop really runs under are derivable (ctx.allowlist EQUALS the names of base_tool_defs(false), or those plus the one server-resolved name tickets.create: exactly the set a resume derives, because a resume requires its derived names to equal the fold's), a round is left (rounds_executed + delegated_rounds < max_rounds, the budget the resume will use) and the run is under the park cap (parks_so_far < MAX_PARKS_PER_RUN). Because it is written at each boundary save, a budget that has run out removes it by itself.

A marked loop also fences a VISIBLE call for the one purpose of removing the marker: a root round that contains a plan.* call (plan.propose writes a plan the user can approve, and is classed Read) or a subagent.* call (it records child runs) first saves the open-round pre_effect fold through save_pre_effect, but only while the writer is Active. The calls are held ONLY when the writer has lost ownership (lost_ownership(), after the attempt): a transient save failure, or a writer that failed for another reason (Refused(KeyUnavailable | Unimplemented), a store that is off), does NOT hold them, because committed code never holds a Read-classed call and a fence must not take plan.propose or a delegation away from a run that has no crash park to protect; they are dispatched as committed code dispatches them (fail open). An open-round fold is never marked, so after a SUCCESSFUL fence a crash after such a call finds an unmarked fold and the run is terminalized as committed; the next end-of-round fold is marked again. After a failed (fail-open) fence a crash parks the marked boundary before the call and a resume repeats it: a second plan proposal (the newest wins) or a second delegation record, benign and listed in the risks. The ledger is not involved. This costs coverage on purpose: a crash inside a delegation round (the longest rounds) or a plan-proposing round is not parkable.

(b) ONE PURE DECISION, TWO CALLERS (mp-contracts). mp_contracts::crash_park::crash_reseal(fold, run_id) -> Result<LoopFoldV1, CrashRefusal> is the only code that decides whether an active fold may become a crash park and what the parked fold is. It refuses (closed tokens, metric labels): a tombstone (not_resumable, which includes the answering and paused tombstones), no marker or a malformed one (strict parse: v 1, non-empty model and connection id, ceiling >= 1), ledger off in the fold, no system prompt first, an open round, a pending pause, a Space, an unpinned authority, a plan_mode that is false or a rung that is not read_only, a payload park count at the cap, a missing prompt digest. It does NOT recompute the round budget (the constants live in execution-core): the marker's presence guarantees a round was left when it was written, and the oracle test pins that at the boundary. It returns the fold with pending = Park{park_ref(run, parks+1)} (no step, call or fingerprint), the S8-A park keys set from the marker's payload (route_model, route_connection_id, child_ceiling, and parks = payload parks + 1) and the marker removed. session-core calls it in production; execution-core calls it in the oracle test that proves "a marked fold re-seals into a fold check_park_fold accepts".

(c) THE WORKER'S CRASH PARK (session-core). In recover_claimed_terminalization, after the S8-A spare and before PARK_HELD_SQL, when crash_park_active(): crash_park_run(tx, cipher, run_id) runs inside the transaction that already holds the run row and the obligation row, then takes the fold row FOR UPDATE. It refuses (the run is terminalized as committed) unless: the run is persistent; a fold exists, is state 'active' and kind round or pre_effect (a park-resume fence is a pre_effect fold with no open round, so the check is by content, not by kind); a cipher is available and opens the fold under AAD seq; check_fold and the row's authority_digest agree with the fold; crash_reseal accepts it; the run has NO row in session_tool_audit_intents (a read-only run reserves nothing, so any row is an anomaly) and NO row in approvals (a read-only run that ever raised an approval is not a run that merely crashed: its fold is stale by construction, and a pinned run saves no pause fold to say so). On success it re-seals under seq+1, kind='park', state='parked', pause_ref = the new park_ref, park_reason 'crash_interrupted', park_until = now() (due at once), a fresh fold_digest, claim fields cleared, updated_at = now() (the TTL anchor), writes the content-free CHECKPOINT_SAVED event like a park save, and the caller hands the obligation back through the S8-A spare at the crash horizon. No terminal state is written.

(d) THE RETIRE FENCE AND THE OWNERSHIP STOP (executor). A marked (armed) loop is about to do one of two things nobody can take back: END THE RUN (finalize: append its answer when retention and the outcome allow it, then write the terminal receipt with a fresh service token) or RAISE AN APPROVAL (pause_for_approval). Immediately before either it calls retire_fence, which uses FoldWriter::retire(counters, authority, reason): one save of a tombstone fold (kind round, extension not_resumable with the closed word "answering" or "paused", no conversation, built from the header alone) at the expected seq, through the same save path as every fold (so the one stale-after-ambiguous probe applies). retire_fence returns Ok at once for a loop that is not armed (its first statement), so an unmarked loop behaves exactly as committed at both sites. There are exactly TWO call sites. (1) One exit funnel in run_rounds: every outcome the loop hands to finalize passes it (Answered, Concluded, Exhausted, OutOfTime, InferFailed with or without a typed refusal, RouteUnavailable), whatever branch produced it: the body after the writer is built becomes an `async` block (a `return` or `?` inside it leaves the block), and the funnel after it passes Err, Paused and Parked through untouched and fences everything else. (2) The pending-pause block: the FIRST statement inside `if let Some(pending) = pending_pause.filter(..)`, before `let offered` and before folds.pause_fold( (a PauseFold holds the writer mutably until pause_for_approval consumes it, so nothing can be placed between the two), with reason "paused".

The decision. A writer that has failed with Stale or Closed (FoldWriter::lost_ownership), or a tombstone save answered Stale or Closed (the seq moved, or the run ended), is POSITIVE evidence that another writer or the run's end got there first: the loop stops, always. The stop is Err(Refusal::ContinueInProgress.status()) (Aborted "continue_in_progress", an existing closed token, never a confirmed rejection), returned before finalize and before the approval exists: no append, no receipt, no approval. Everything else is UNPROVEN, not lost: a Denied or NotFound answer (the store will not let this loop write: for a loop silent past its 300 s session bearer that is the ordinary answer, because the bearer is checked before the seq; a writer already Failed with one of them is FoldWriter::ownership_unproven), a transient failure (Unavailable, a timeout; retried twice more, 100 ms then 300 ms, only for Unavailable and only while the writer is still Active), and an exhausted retry. An unproven exit is resolved with the executor's OWN MONOTONIC CLOCK: the loop remembers when it last SENT a heartbeat that the store accepted (the run-level heartbeat before run_rounds, then each loop-top heartbeat; a heartbeat error already ends the loop). The worker cannot crash-park a run before its obligation's deadline_at, which is the heartbeat's receipt time plus 900 s, and the receipt is never earlier than the send, so while that age is under the fail-open window (900 s minus a 120 s skew margin, constants in mp-contracts pinned to session-core's MANAGED_DEADLINE_SECS) no crash park can exist and no resume can be running: the loop proceeds (committed behaviour, fail open: parking upgrades a failure, it is never a new way to fail). At or past the window the loop stops. So a superseded loop silent past the deadline always stops, whatever the transport does, and a healthy loop whose bearers expired a moment ago still writes its immediate failed receipt as committed code does. At every loop top, before anything else, an armed loop stops when its writer has lost ownership, or when it is unproven and the heartbeat age is at or past the window, so a loop whose end-of-round save was refused does not run the rest of its budget.

try_park's two close paths are NOT alike. After NotAccepted the row at the seq the store answered is this loop's own park row (the store returned Ok for the loop's own save at the expected seq), which the loop closes best effort: the writer becomes Off(SelfClosed) and the loop finalizes as committed (a closed row can neither be crash-parked nor claimed; if the close does not land, the stray row is a typed park that finalize's terminal transition purges, as in S8-A; the one race, a claimant on a replica with park recovery on fencing the row before the close lands while replicas disagree about park recovery, is S8-A's own exposure and is listed in the matrix). After an AMBIGUOUS park save a marked loop does NOT close expected+1 and does NOT mark itself closed: that seq may hold the worker's crash park (a re-seal lands at exactly the seq a park save would use), and closing it would destroy a park the loop does not own. It leaves the writer Active, so the funnel's tombstone settles ownership: if the loop's own park committed, the tombstone's one probe overwrites it and the loop proceeds; if an UNCLAIMED crash park sits there, the probe takes the run back (a live loop with a valid bearer is an owner: exactly one proceeds, section 2); if a resume already fenced, Stale and the loop stops; with a dead bearer the answer is Denied and the age rule decides. An unmarked loop keeps S8-A's close unchanged. A writer that is not Active and is not unproven (off, a NotAccepted self-close, or failed for a non-ownership reason such as KeyUnavailable), or one with no authority to write under, makes no RPC and proceeds at once, with no retry sleep. The tombstone before a pause also removes a stale marked boundary fold from a run that is now waiting on an approval; if that save is not confirmed, crash_park_run's approvals fact still refuses the run.

(e) RESUME IS S8-A, UNCHANGED. A claim by the empty reference takes a due kind=park row; the executor's check_park_fold accepts the fold; route_request rebuilds the pinned route from the identifiers; the fence at the claimed seq ends any zombie; the resumed loop is itself marked (seed Parked), so it can be crash-parked again, up to the cap.

(f) A SEPARATE, SHORTER TTL. The S8-A spare horizon uses the park TTL for every kind=park fold. S8-B parameterizes it: a fold whose park_reason is crash_interrupted uses crash_park_ttl_seconds() (default 3600, clamp 60 s to 30 days; the total wait of an unresumed crash park is the 15-minute deadline PLUS this TTL, so no value matches the committed 15 minutes). The crash TTL is bound whenever park_recovery_active(), independent of SESSION_CORE_CRASH_PARK_ENABLED: killing the crash switch must never make existing crash parks fall back to the 24-hour park TTL. Nothing else about the spare changes.

2. WHY THE SAFETY ARGUMENT IS EXACT (the part v4 did not have)

What can a read-only marked loop A do that anyone can see? It cannot run an effect: the rung gate rejects every call the ledger classes as effectful, at every depth, and plan mode rejects risky calls. It can read, infer, spend subscription quota, publish informational events, write a plan proposal (plan.propose is classed Read, writes a plan the user can approve, and is superseded by the next proposal) and delegation records (subagent.*), and do the acts nobody can take back: end the run (an appended answer and the terminal receipt) and raise an approval (the only calls a read-only run can still be gated on are the disclosure-consent read, read_subagent_result, which asks on every posture, and a read a capability policy chooses to ask about; a pinned run's approval is cold). So duplicate EFFECTS are impossible by construction; the two visible writes that are not effects (a plan proposal, a delegation) are fenced by the pre_effect fold of section 1(a): a crash after one finds an unmarked fold, and a stale loop's save for it is refused so the call is held; and the acts that matter, ending the run and raising an approval, are fenced by the retire tombstone.

Let R be the commit of A's tombstone save (before it ends the run or raises an approval) and P the commit of the worker's re-seal. Both are transactions that take the runs row lock first (run_owner_tx in save, lock_run_status in the worker) and then the fold row lock, so they are totally ordered.
- If P < R: A's tombstone save expects the old seq, finds seq+1 and answers aborted "loop_checkpoint_stale" (or closed/run_ended if the run ended): A stops and appends, receipts and raises nothing. The one exception is the stale-after-ambiguous probe: a loop whose EARLIER save timed out has one retry at seq+1, and that can match the re-sealed seq. Then A wins the compare-and-set against a resume that has not fenced yet, A owns the run again (the park is undone) and the resume's fence is the stale one. Either way exactly one of A and B proceeds, because both are saves at the same seq.
- If R < P: the worker reads a tombstone: crash_reseal refuses (not_resumable). The run is terminalized as committed. A's append, receipt or approval happens after R, so it can only land on a run that is never crash-parked.
Therefore: if A appends an answer, writes a receipt or raises an approval, the fold was already a tombstone, and the run is never crash-parked afterwards; if the run is crash-parked, neither ever happens from A. A crash after the append and before the receipt leaves a tombstone, so the worker terminalizes (outcome_unknown, as committed) and nothing resumes a run that already answered.

The second, independent fact: a loop silent for 900 s holds session, inference and data-plane bearers that expired at 300 s (by default). Every session-core write it can still try is refused Unauthenticated before any seq is compared, which the loop reads as Refused(Denied). That is why a Denied answer cannot be read as 'not stale', and why the funnel resolves it with the age rule: a zombie silent past the deadline has a last accepted heartbeat older than 900 s, so it stops; its terminal receipt uses a fresh service token (so does its heartbeat), so WITHOUT the funnel a zombie that failed typed (InferFailed, RouteUnavailable) would have terminalized the crash park through the service token while B ran. With a PLANE_TOKEN_TTL longer than the deadline the bearers do not expire and the seq compare-and-set is what stops it. The age rule is the third: it uses no store answer at all, only the executor's own clock, so it holds even when every RPC of a woken zombie fails at the transport. The design needs all three and relies on none alone.

The residual is stated, not hidden, and it is the HEALTHY loop's, not the zombie's: if the tombstone save keeps failing for a transient reason (three attempts) while the loop's last heartbeat is recent, it proceeds (fail open, as every fold save is). If the append then succeeds and the receipt fails, or the executor crashes in the gap, the run is left non-terminal with its marked fold stored; a crash park at the next deadline and a user continuing would then give a second answer. It needs three consecutive tombstone failures on the same service that then answers the append, plus a failed receipt or a crash, plus a resume: every step is a service failure, correlated only by a flapping replica. Closing it exactly needs a durable fact ordered with the append (the v4 thread watermark: a migration and a claim that takes the thread lock); rejected for v1 with that cost, listed for the owner.

A loop that is alive but silent for 15 minutes and is crash-parked while it works: it can no longer fold-save, it stops at its next loop top, and it can neither end the run nor raise an approval (the tombstone is refused or Denied). It may waste read-only work. Its next heartbeat renews the obligation exactly as today; that is harmless because the fold is a park and the spare handles it. A live loop that DOES heartbeat in time is not parked at all: a heartbeat between the worker's claim and its recovery transaction turns the obligation back to 'open', and the worker's existing check (state must still be 'processing' with its own lease) abandons the pass.

3. THE FIVE DEFECTS, ONE BY ONE

(1) UNRESUMABLE RUNS HELD FOR THE TTL.
Cause in v2 to v4: the marker was set from a tool listing, or from the request of a continuation, so a client-tool run, an approval continuation, or a run whose snapshot tools came from a pause could carry it.
Now: (i) marked only if crash_marking_active() and the static facts hold, which include tools_derivable by REQUEST KIND (offered_tools never calls a resume request derivable, and run_agent_with_route_parkable ands it with "no release"), seed Fresh or Parked and no release: three independent facts that each exclude an approval continuation, segment or in place; (ii) NEW: the tool names are proven from the loop's own allowlist, which is built AFTER a continuation's snapshot replaced the request's tools, so even a future code path that let a continuation through the first three facts would fail here when its snapshot held a client or MCP tool; (iii) the re-seal is refused without the marker, and crash_reseal re-derives every structural fact check_park_fold needs except the round budget (which the marker's write condition guarantees and the oracle test pins at the boundary); (iv) the oracle test builds a marked fold, re-seals it and runs check_park_fold on it; (v) the residual (deploy or config drift between crash and resume: prompt digest, tool names, a route that no longer resolves) is bounded by the deadline plus the crash TTL, not the park TTL; (vi) an unmarked fold is terminalized at the committed deadline, which a DB test pins for each excluded class. What is excluded, honestly: every SPA turn that sends client tool specs, every run in an org with an MCP tool, every effect-capable run.

(2) A LIVE-BUT-SILENT LOOP PARKED UNDER ITSELF: see section 2. The four facts the review named: save_round is best effort (a marked loop reads lost_ownership at the next loop top and stops); a stale save only fails the writer (it now also ends a marked loop); the heartbeat ignores already_terminal (the funnel's Closed covers an ended run, so the loop never appends to one or writes its receipt; the heartbeat is left alone); append_message_in_tx locks only threads (nothing waits on it: the append is ordered AFTER the tombstone by the fold row, and the worker never queries messages). A zombie raising an approval card, or terminalizing the run through its service-token receipt after a typed failure, is fenced the same way: the funnel stands before finalize, not before the append.

(3) THE CLOCKS: no comparison of messages.created_at (Utc::now() in append_message_in_tx) with run_loop_checkpoints.updated_at (database now()) anywhere. The one place two timestamps meet is none: the worker uses no timestamp at all in its decision (the ledger and approval facts are "any row", not "after the fold"), and the park's due time and TTL are database now() on both sides.

(4) OLD SESSION-CORE, NEW EXECUTOR: section 1(d) and the failure matrix. The executor learns nothing from the store; it writes data an old store ignores. The tombstone save is a valid fold save an old store accepts, and an old store has no crash park, so nothing can resume a run behind the tombstone. A store without S8-B never re-seals. The reverse (S8-B store, executor without marks) never re-seals either. There is no coupled flip to get wrong.

(5) THE KILL AND ROLLBACK ORDER: section 6.

4. EXECUTOR DETAILS

mp-contracts crash_park.rs also carries the two constants the executor's age rule needs: MANAGED_DEADLINE_SECS (900) and FAIL_OPEN_MARGIN_SECS (120), and `fail_open_window() -> Duration`; a session-core unit test pins MANAGED_DEADLINE_SECS equal to terminalization.rs's constant.

park.rs gains: crash_marking_active(); CrashStaticFacts {base: StaticFacts, read_only, tool_names_derivable}; run_crash_parkable(), an INDEPENDENT function with its own explicit conjunct list (the same facts as run_parkable minus parking_active(), plus read_only and tool_names_derivable): run_parkable and park_fold are NOT touched, so S8-A's guards on their text, the park-key rule and the mutation rows of the park-writer guard test stay as committed, and a unit test pins that for every combination of facts run_parkable (with parking on) and run_crash_parkable (with marking on, read_only and names true) agree; crash_ineligible_reason(facts) -> Option<&'static str> (the first failing fact as a closed word, used only for the one per-run info! line); crash_marked(CrashFacts{run_crash_parkable, rounds_used, max_rounds, parks_so_far}); crash_mark_of(route, child_ceiling, parks) -> mp_contracts::crash_park::CrashMark (the payload type and its to_value live in mp-contracts, the single producer and parser); tool_names_derivable(allowlist); is_visible_call(name) (plan.* or subagent.*); `must_stop(folds, armed, heartbeat_age) -> bool` (pure: armed && (lost_ownership || (ownership_unproven && heartbeat_age >= fail_open_window()))), used by the loop top and by retire_fence; CRASH_RETIRE_BUILT and its test override. try_park (S8B-3) gains `if !folds.is_fenced() { close_stray_park(..) }` in its ambiguous arm and `folds.note_self_closed()` after the close of its NotAccepted arm only.

fold.rs, S8B-2: Snapshot gains ONE field, `crash_mark: Option<&CrashMark>` (the parks count rides inside the mark); build_fold writes the marker, under the key lf::EXT_CRASH_PARKABLE and through CrashMark::to_value, only when crash_mark is Some AND round is None AND pending is None; FoldWriter gains the pure accessors arm_fence() / is_fenced() (a flag set once when the run's CrashGate is armed), lost_ownership() (State::Failed(Stale | Closed)) and ownership_unproven() (State::Failed(Refused(Denied | NotFound))). fold.rs, S8B-3: OffReason gains SelfClosed ("self_closed"); FoldWriter gains note_self_closed() (state := Off(SelfClosed)) and retire(counters, authority, reason) (a writer that is not Active makes no call and returns Unavailable{ambiguous:false}; otherwise it builds the Header from its own run id and segment plus the arguments and calls the existing save_tombstone_as(header, reason, SaveKind::Round, ""), so it shares the save_bytes path and its stale probe; reason is one of two closed words). retire never builds a park: it cannot name SaveKind::Park. It writes one content-free info! line per call (target loop_fold, retire=reason, outcome).

agent.rs: LoopContext gains `heartbeat_floor: Instant`, taken immediately BEFORE the run-level heartbeat in run_agent_with_route_parkable; run_rounds keeps `last_beat`, initialised from it and set to Instant::now() taken immediately BEFORE each loop-top heartbeat that returns Ok. A CrashGate {armed: bool, mark: CrashMark, max_rounds: u32, parks_so_far: u32} is built ONCE before the loop from park::run_crash_parkable (read-only from req, names from ctx.allowlist); its parks_so_far is COPIED from park_gate.parks_so_far, never re-spelled from the seed (the park-writer guard's mutation rows anchor on that expression and would otherwise mutate the wrong literal); mark_for(rounds_used) returns Some(&mark) only when park::crash_marked says so; the loop arms the writer (folds.arm_fence()) when the gate is armed; one info! line per run says whether marking is armed and, when not, the first failing fact (the evidence for the owner's flip gate, because execution-core records no metrics). The five production Snapshot literals gain the one field (park_at_boundary's passes None so a park fold is untouched; the park-resume fence snapshot, the open-round and the end-of-round snapshots pass crash_gate.mark_for(rounds_used); the pause snapshot is built with an open round and, for a pinned run, never built at all because the pause is cold, so build_fold's rule refuses a marker there by construction). The visible-call fence (S8B-2): the count of plan.* and subagent.* calls of a round is added to effectful_calls for an armed loop whose writer is Active, so the existing save_pre_effect decision runs for them; a local `let visible_hold = crash_gate.armed && folds.lost_ownership();` is computed once after that save and before join_all (the dispatch closures are `async move` and capture only Copy values and references, never the FoldWriter, and ownership cannot change in phase 2 because nothing is saved there), and the dispatch closure's own hold is `visible_hold && park::is_visible_call(&call.name)` (NOT `effects_held`: a Read-classed call has no ledger entry, so the committed hold never applied to it). The committed `note_effects_held` count and warn cover effectful calls only; a round whose only visible calls were dispatched after a failed fence gets its own info! line. The loop-top check (S8B-3) is `must_stop(&folds, crash_gate.armed, last_beat.elapsed())`; the exit funnel and the pending-pause retire of 1(d) go through ONE helper, retire_fence(folds, crash_gate, counters, authority: Option<&Authority>, reason, site, heartbeat_age) -> Result<(), Status>, which returns Ok at once when the gate is not armed, then applies the decision of 1(d), re-checking is_active() before every retry sleep, and is the ONLY caller of folds.retire. The funnel recognises Paused and Parked with `matches!(result.outcome, RoundsOutcome::Paused(_) | RoundsOutcome::Parked(_))` and never with an `outcome:` field pattern (the park-writer guard counts `outcome: RoundsOutcome::Parked(` literals). The funnel's tombstone header takes its counters from the RoundsResult (rounds_executed, delegated_rounds) and the loop's step_seq, and the authority from fold_authority, so it needs no boundary fold and adds no Snapshot literal. run_rounds keeps its signature; only its body moves into the async block, so no caller changes. The async block re-indents the loop (rustfmt is a CI check), which breaks the whitespace of committed mutation rows (the Site B ParkPoint anchor in the park-writer guard test, which has 20 leading spaces, and two rows, one in each meta-test, that pass only by substring luck): S8B-3 re-anchors them whitespace-insensitively in the same commit (see file_changes).

With the switch off, or with CRASH_RETIRE_BUILT false, crash_mark is None everywhere and the gate is not armed: every fold is byte-identical to S8-A's, no retire RPC is made, no ownership stop exists, the funnel and the pause-site call are the identity. Observation: execution-core records no metrics, so the evidence of the marker, the tombstone and a stop is content-free info! lines with closed fields (the per-run armed line, retire=answering|paused, superseded site=loop_top|exit|pause|unproven, outcome), and the counters are best effort for the day a recorder exists.

5. SESSION-CORE DETAILS

terminalization.rs: CRASH_PARK_ENV, crash_park_enabled() with a thread-local override (the existing pattern), crash_park_active() = park_recovery_active() && crash_park_enabled(); PARK_SPARE_HORIZON_SQL keeps its shape and gains two binds, `$4` the crash TTL and `$5` the reason token (bound from ParkReason::CrashInterrupted.as_str(), so the string is never spelled in terminalization.rs), in a CASE on park_reason in both places the wait appears ($1 run, $2 park TTL, $3 deadline period, $4 crash TTL, $5 token), bound whenever park_recovery_active() whatever the crash switch says; PARK_HELD_SQL is replaced by a query that returns the held fold's park_reason (the metric label, mapped through ParkReason::parse), behaviour unchanged; the worker branch; a worker_cipher() that is DescriptorCipher::from_env().ok() in production and a test key under a test override; metrics in two families so the counter reads: mp_session_loop_crash_park_skipped_total{reason} for the ordinary non-candidates (not_persistent, no_fold, not_active, kind and a tombstone or unmarked fold: NotResumable, NotMarked), and mp_session_loop_crash_park_unreadable_total{reason} for the operational failures that hide what the fold was (key_unavailable, did_not_authenticate, unreadable, authority_digest), and mp_session_loop_crash_park_refused_total{reason} for a candidate that carried a marker and was refused (every other store-level or CrashRefusal reason), plus mp_session_loop_crash_parks_total.
loop_checkpoint.rs: crash_park_ttl_seconds() with its own env and clamp; CrashParkOutcome {Parked{seq}, Refused(&'static str)}; crash_park_run(tx, cipher, run_id); the SQL constants. It lives here because scope_aad, sha256_hex and record_event are private to this file (check_fold is pub(crate)).
Lock order is unchanged: runs, obligation, fold. save and claim already take runs then fold; the worker holds runs and the obligation from before the branch; nothing in the branch takes the thread row, an approvals row or a ledger row with a lock (the two facts are plain SELECT EXISTS).
The transaction decrypts and encrypts up to 4 MiB while holding the three locks: acceptable at crash frequency, the same trade the S8 designs already accepted.

6. SWITCHES, FLIP ORDER, KILL ORDER, ROLLBACK

Prerequisites for flipping anything of S8-B: S8-A flips (1) the S8-0 expiry switch, (2) park recovery, (3) the gateway and BFF park form are on, and the S8-A park resume has been live-proven (stage s8a-C). The executor's EXECUTION_LOOP_PARKING_ENABLED (S8-A flip 4) is NOT a prerequisite: a resume is never gated by it.
Flip: (5) EXECUTION_LOOP_CRASH_PARK_ENABLED in execution-core (with the resume switches already on); (6) SESSION_CORE_CRASH_PARK_ENABLED in session-core. BOTH are owner-gated, and so is their ORDER. (5) first is the proposal: it lets the owner read the per-run armed line before session-core acts, but it is not observation-only (see below). (6) first is the alternative: with no marked folds session-core refuses every fold as NotMarked, so it is dormant until (5), and (5)'s effects are then borne only when crash-park is meant to work. What (5) changes for a MARKED run: a loop that meets a run that ended under it (a cancel, a worker terminalization, a duplicate dispatch) or that the store refuses (Denied) now stops without a late answer or receipt, and one extra tombstone RPC per run end (up to about 6 s of retries when session-core is degraded), and a pre_effect save before a plan or delegation round (up to the 10 s gated timeout plus one probe when session-core is degraded). A marked loop that overran its credentials but whose last heartbeat is recent keeps the committed immediate failed receipt (the age rule). Those effects are part of the owner's flip decision.
Kill: (6) off first (no new crash park; existing ones stay spared until the CRASH TTL, claimable, resumable, the TTL still the crash TTL); (5) off (no new marks, no tombstone, no ownership stop). A switch is container environment, so flipping (5) means recreating the executor container: every in-flight loop dies with it and is crash-parked at its deadline if (6) is still on, so (6) goes off FIRST whatever order the owner chose to flip them in; the same holds for any execution-core or model-gateway restart or deploy while both are on. Then the S8-A order. While a crash park exists NONE of the resume lane may go off first: the executor's resume switches, the gateway's own AGENT_CONTINUE_AFTER_APPROVAL_ENABLED and EXECUTION_LOOP_RESUME_ENABLED (model-gateway reads both; the committed S7 text rolls the GATEWAY back first, and S8-B overrides that order while parks exist), the gateway or BFF park form (an image below S8-7 or S8-8 included) or an executor image below S8-A; otherwise those parks are held for the crash TTL and cannot be resumed.
Rollback of images: session-core below S8-B only after (6) is off and the crash parks have drained (wait the crash TTL or cancel the runs): an S8-A session-core spares a crash park for the park TTL (24 h by default) and knows no crash TTL. During a rolling deploy replicas of different images share the worker queue, so whichever replica claims an obligation decides and an S8-A replica can spare a crash park under the park TTL; replicas whose switches or descriptor key differ decide differently too: drain before rolling. Execution-core below S8-B: marks stop; existing crash parks stay resumable by the S8-A executor. Execution-core below S8-A: never while a crash park exists.
Deploy order: session-core first (dormant), then execution-core (one image holding both executor steps).

7. WHAT WAS DROPPED FROM v4, AND WHY

- The thread watermark and migration 0049: not needed once the answer is fenced by the fold's own seq; it also needed the claim to take the thread lock and was the last open clock argument.
- loop_checkpoint_superseded and the segment comparison in save: the executor stops on a definitive Stale or Closed, which the store already says; a store-side classification added a token, a SaveError variant, SQL and a rule per lineage for a distinction a marked loop does not need.
- fence_answer as a fold SAVE of the live conversation: replaced by a tombstone (no conversation retained, and a tombstone is exactly "do not resume from here").
- A crash grace and the heartbeat push: with read-only runs and the exact answer fence a live loop cannot do harm after the re-seal, so the grace bought only latency. The re-seal is due at once.
- A heartbeat rejection while parked: considered; it stops a zombie one round earlier but changes a hot, guarded function for no safety gain.
- A ReserveToolAction fence and the ledger-after-fold timestamp fact: only needed for effect-capable runs, which are out of v1.
- A third writer_gate: the park-resume gate already exists (S8-A park_continuation); S8-B adds no writer.
- Writing the S8-A park keys (parks, route_model, route_connection_id, child_ceiling) on every boundary fold through a shared mark_extensions and splitting run_parkable (a first draft of this design): both turn committed S8-A guards and their mutation rows red. One payload key and an independent run_crash_parkable leave them untouched.
- A background liveness beat in the executor (so that "silent 15 minutes" means "dead"): the right long-term fix for false death, but it changes the heartbeat contract of every run and needs a hung-loop bound; listed as an optional follow-up.
- A store-side refusal of an ExecutionAgent terminal receipt while the fold is a crash park (a reviewer's suggestion): it would cover only the unclaimed window (after a resume's fence nothing distinguishes the zombie's receipt from B's), and the funnel already stands before finalize; listed as a defense in depth, not built.
'''
